  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "A math teacher split their calculus class into two groups, Group $A$ and Group $B.$ Group $A$ has $43%$ of the total number of students, while Group $B$ has $57%$ of the total number of students. If $23%$ of the students in Group $A$ obtained the top grade, and $19%$ of the students in Group $B$ obtained the top grade, what is the probability that a randomly selected student from either group obtained the top grade?",
  "worked_solution": "Let $G$ be the event that the chosen student obtained the top grade, and let $A$ be the event that the student is from Group $A.$ Then, ${A}^{\u2032}$ is the event that the student is from Group $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected student obtained the top grade, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(G)=P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|{A}^{\u2032})P({A}^{\u2032})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $43%$ of their students are in Group $A$ and $57%$ are in Group $B$, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.43,\\,P({A}^{\u2032})=0.57.\n$$\n\n- Since $23%$ of the students in Group $A$ obtained the top grade and $19%$ of the students in Group $B$ obtained the top grade, we have\n\n$$\nP(G|A)=0.23,\\,P(G|{A}^{\u2032})=0.19.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected student from either group obtained the top grade\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G) & =P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|{A}^{\u2032})P({A}^{\u2032}) \\\\  & =(0.23)(0.43)+(0.19)(0.57) \\\\  & \u22480.207\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.",
